  "summary": "Counterculture visual language of melting letterforms, vibrating complementary colors and kaleidoscopic liquefaction.",
  "description": "Born with LSD culture, the Fillmore posters of Wes Wilson, Victor Moscoso and Rick Griffin warped Art Nouveau-derived lettering until words melted, paired clashing complements for vibration, and swirled Art Nouveau, Vienna Secession and Op Art into concerts posters, album covers and light shows. It spread through psychedelic rock — Hendrix, Beatles, Jefferson Airplane — into fashion and film titles, then dissolved; its biology of distortion keeps resurfacing in rave flyers, vaporwave and festival visuals.",
